#{[{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101688} :activity/id #uuid "ba74d421-fd3e-5b54-a25f-af656df35759" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101688 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101963 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have two vectors $\\textbf{u}=\\langle 1, 3 \\rangle$ and $\\textbf{v}=\\langle 2,-1 \\rangle.$ The parallelogram spanned by $\\textbf{u}$ and $\\textbf{v}$ is shown in the picture below:\n\n![](images/10/102f72ba6730d1b07ca0d01186a1ff7851954193f00dc74eb7150981844b0222.png)\n\nNow, we construct a new matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors. Let's find the determinant of $M\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt turns out that the absolute value of $\\det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|det(M)| \\\\  & =|-7| \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "90a7caf1-54f7-517e-93cb-98a6d39494ad" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3014 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e1acb842-d646-50c3-b138-c3d25f4aec2f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101689}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101689 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100878 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "288e49b0-f706-54de-86b7-a17248fe2408"} :step/id #uuid "a915d8c2-6eb6-5077-9e2e-597348800c3d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101700}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101690 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100879 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0272b8d6-8400-5a6d-b51d-fc6ca84bd166"} :step/id #uuid "ddbb79fe-cd5e-51a7-a873-1eb6188a8325" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101691}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101691 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101964 :tutorial/content "Why does the absolute value of the determinant represent the area of the parallelogram? Let's prove this result.\n\nFirst, let's state the result precisely. Consider two vectors $\\textbf{u}=\\langle a,b \\rangle$ and $\\textbf{v}=\\langle c,d \\rangle.$ The parallelogram spanned by $\\textbf{u}$ and $\\textbf{v}$ is shown in the picture below:\n\n![](images/3a/3a466fd3c52ea345d80b9ba67ac104efef29bfd7eeed0e4567364c80f3212c0d.png)\n\nWe construct a new matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a & c \\\\ b & d\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors. Then the absolute value of $\\det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram spanned by $\\textbf{u}$ and $\\textbf{v}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nA = |\\det(M)|\n$$\n\nTo prove the formula, we use the following picture:\n\n![](images/3d/3da110b114d2038738bd4ef06cb031bb8b66aee062ffc83955dfdaade72584e1.png)\n\nWe can compute the area by starting with the area of the outer rectangle, and then subtracting off the area of the smaller triangles and rectangles surrounding the parallelogram:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|(a+c)(b+d)-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}cd-2\\cdot ad-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}ab| \\\\  & =|ab+ad+bc+cd-cd-2ad-ab| \\\\  & =|bc-ad| \\\\  & =|ad-bc| \\\\  & =|det(M)|\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ca25b302-10d5-5d18-a10f-0c50af28db94" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3015 :tutorial/title "General Rule"} :step/id #uuid "222ac5de-6fe2-55b8-a6e5-18988da71e89"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101700 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101966 :tutorial/content "When two vectors are parallel, the parallelogram does not exist. So, it has zero area.\n\n![](images/b6/b65c64ccf16764f99c0c30dbf6b37ce7b6254b83496512a5d207dddab095dac7.png)\n\nAs a result, the determinant of a matrix whose columns are parallel vectors must also be zero. \n\nFor example, in the diagram above, we have the vectors $\\textbf{u} = \\left< 1,1 \\right>$ and $\\textbf{v} = \\left< 2,2 \\right>.$ Constructing a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIndeed, the determinant does come out to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ff830819-9cbf-5fb9-852c-f885f8cd6d68" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3173 :tutorial/title "The Case When Two Vectors are Parallel"} :step/id #uuid "ba53472f-59c9-53a7-bc81-a3233068d585" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101690}}] :activity/title "The Geometric Interpretation of the 2x2 Determinant" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186101036}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186048857 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100878 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100700 :question/id #uuid "fe75d710-2f18-5ddb-9f93-3127d8cec7ed" :question/math-academy-id "e-4437" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\textbf{u}=\\langle 1,2 \\rangle$ and $\\textbf{v}=\\langle 3, -3 \\rangle.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n![](images/f4/f4b59236b774117dfc764389f3525a01c5f4c8429cece16552622f54cfb3d863.png)\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-3)-3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $\\det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA = |{-9}| = 9\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "288e49b0-f706-54de-86b7-a17248fe2408" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101150 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "979ba87c-1473-5f9b-8799-6d3912540795" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098236} {:answer/id #uuid "1120b8d9-ef11-5002-97fb-73b8cdca4f70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098237} {:answer/id #uuid "12f