)+C$, if\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$B=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 4 \\\\ 7 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 9 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify the expression by distributing the subtraction over the matrices in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-C)-(A-B)+C & =A-C-A+B+C \\\\  & =\\cancel{A}-\\cancel{C}-\\cancel{A}+B+\\cancel{C} \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left with a single matrix. So, the result is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 4 \\\\ 7 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567291 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b4fc929-293d-5895-977b-c8759cf171c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567273} {:answer/id #uuid "2e64014f-3d91-5cee-9ba1-9bb7f1005c0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567277} {:answer/id #uuid "7b60ea78-2219-5b19-8e2c-edcde565621e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 8 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567287} {:answer/id #uuid "d381b82d-0da0-5fb1-9ced-71db6fa75315" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567305} {:answer/id #uuid "e41bd865-32e8-5435-ba1b-78a0af3b1ff4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567307}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 8 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567287} :answer-field/id #uuid "4341a1bc-187a-5b45-a0a4-790be36bbd49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567278}] :question/id #uuid "94dff20e-185a-57ca-bb97-908a1c4e39c9" :question/problem "Find $A-(C+A)+(C+B)$, if\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$B=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 8 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$C=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify the expression by distributing the subtraction over the matrices in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(C+A)+(C+B) & =A-C-A+C+B \\\\  & =\\cancel{A}-\\cancel{C}-\\cancel{A}+\\cancel{C}+B \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left with a single matrix. So, the result is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 8 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567300 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53479d87-1b6b-5f9b-a5e1-e4e4b4dfd780" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567280} {:answer/id #uuid "62cdc045-df33-5983-9a59-f6eab2cc0ad3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 9 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567284} {:answer/id #uuid "8c987bf8-3e9b-52b2-a475-f56191bc58f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567290} {:answer/id #uuid "9db5edfc-1d4a-5dec-ab30-b34fc393e43c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567295} {:answer/id #uuid "ad5f5a26-6d9c-537f-bee8-976a327b96d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567297}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567295} :answer-field/id #uuid "8464e939-249e-5366-b15a-dfebfc20f655" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567289}] :question/id #uuid "b6e4ccf4-6e29-5d0e-abca-2b9461a5e403" :question/problem "Find $(B+A)-(C+B)+C$, if\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$B=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$C=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 9 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify the expression by distributing the subtraction over the matrices in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(B+A)-(C+B)+C & =B+A-C-B+C \\\\  & =\\cancel{B}+A-\\cancel{C}-\\cancel{B}+\\cancel{C} \\\\  & =A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left with a single matrix. So, the result is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567306 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "146ba6fd-65d9-5eac-b8c6-753054ca734e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567272} {:answer/id #uuid "7eb870e3-d126-56da-900f-58c9c4848f19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567288} {:answer/id #uuid "9da2ef4f-06e3-59ac-b555-66a1c6ce062d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567294} {:answer/id #uuid "acb4f8a3-c7cf-5ba0-8238-0eaa18cd8b57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 7 \\\\ 8 & 8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567296} {:answer/id #uuid "fcf76bdd-b7e9-5fd2-bea9-c77260928a82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567311}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567272} :answer-field/id #uuid "9900aac4-ff32-5fdc-b53a-b18af36448de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567292}] :question/id #uuid "d8eb829a-4bbf-5052-a5c5-a62f628308c4" :question/problem "Find $A-(B-C)-(A-B)$, if\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 7 \\\\ 8 & 8\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$C=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify the expression by distributing the subtraction over the matrices in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(B-C)-(A-B) & =A-B+C-A+B \\\\  & =\\cancel{A}-\\cancel{B}+C-\\cancel{A}+\\cancel{B} \\\\  & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left with a single matrix. So, the result is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567308 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f5b833b-df7c-5eef-bca2-28f5c24a267c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567274} {:answer/id #uuid "2c1e9f5d-3b48-58f5-a254-2137eba84337" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567276} {:answer/id #uuid "5699b8d4-7df2-5bf6-b12b-187487650011" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567282} {:answer/id #uuid "74349aad-386e-52d2-bc29-66434f664eb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567286} {:answer/id #uuid "992a3789-8aa2-519a-824a-5d7283c1a729" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567293}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567282} :answer-field/id #uuid "01d232a7-1a9f-57a0-865a-b4694b2441d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567270}] :question/id #uuid "e5acf887-8d5b-5d86-8b2d-418ab46a7a7a" :question/problem "Find $(B+A)-(B-C)-A$, if\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$C=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify the expression by distributing the subtraction over the matrices in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(B+A)-(B-C)-A & =B+A-B+C-A \\\\  & =\\cancel{B}+\\cancel{A}-\\cancel{B}+C-\\cancel{A} \\\\  & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left with a single matrix. So, the result is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Sum or Difference of Three or More Matrices Using Distributivity"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1168 :topic/title "Properties of Matrix Addition"}] [{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084035} :activity/id #uuid "ae967f6f-d65d-58ab-92c5-919873a0041a" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084035 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084506 :tutorial/content "Recall that the **principle of induction** states the following: \n\n*Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1), \\, P(2), \\, P(3), \\, \\ldots,$ where*\n\n- *$P(1)$ is true, and*\n\n- *$P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in\\mathbb N.$*\n\n*Then, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in\\mathbb N.$*\n\nLet's recall our general strategy for proving results using induction:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this lesson, we'll learn how induction can be used to prove matrix identities." :tutorial/id #uuid "a5a687c3-df1f-5c1e-ade0-95d0b1a59130" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7936 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3dcabe33-0b1b-5b19-a8b5-c9eb06bef1dd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084036}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084036 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084520 :tutorial/content "Let's prove the following result using mathematical induction:\n\n![](images/f9/f9106bf23db3c56425757ea9b8b3aef7adf5cbe926212746c85c63e4193f308d.svg) for all $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n![](images/82/82fb358f5e84e37e035da6cfa101c65f6e43b9bad75c63299a56999b125c7f63.svg)\n\nWe start by proving the base case, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that they're equal:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2(1) \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\geq 1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\geq 1,$ we have\n![](images/7e/7e1416be5b4270fdfe3051c74f1db5106556ed860c9f88fcbdd866f48ff3339b.svg)\n\nWe must show that $P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the **inductive step**.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n![](images/e0/e0042401a1070f9f1977a7005f6875f8bc14886842a908fcc78f33678fdcb39b.svg)\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nWhen proving matrix identities using induction, the key to unlocking the proof is to factor out one instance of the matrix, as follows: \n\n![](images/06/06dc062c2c15fba27384ac9ec89ddbe2827ea1901ed74ccef1f97dcf11991d54.svg)\n\nLet's apply this technique in this case:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k+1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2k \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2+2k \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2(k+1) \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ equals the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction,\n![](images/2a/2a3c4d7370e4036aea2b0ad07bdee4a244ea828bef025178c8919be08a410b23.svg)\nis true for all $n \\in \\mathbb{N}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "de68c3b6-b164-5e23-8be7-7ae241373e6c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9171 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "d59caf81-e708-5ba7-9708-465ba28d6f0d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084037}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084037 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083176 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a47ce42d-7fbb-5f7c-999d-4c276632310b"} :step/id #uuid "173260d2-c554-5819-bbfa-79ccd13ba1fe" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084038}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084038 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083177 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6befd731-de3f-545c-aca4-b51b67c68ace"} :step/id #uuid "5de694e9-4e93-5ae1-94ad-4019024f665b"}] :activity/title "Proving Matrix Identities Using Induction" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186083462}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186048869 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083176 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082926 :question/id #uuid "5fb13d73-4955-5ae4-bb31-bec3aef3cca9" :question/math-academy-id "e-13237" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n![](images/4e/4eb97ebf3c509832a78f507427a4dead8caf2e495528121f29caf4f1e6b78882.svg)\n\nWhat are the missing entries in the proof template below?\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n![](images/da/da5c94a138a2a1c37b4eca700822c11d1740bc7f191272bd7b04eb4f0331bf36.svg)\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some ![](images/fb/fb9a89f02c5d1f70a8213f78ab487122536a4488f27a191baddf7cc11e0190ae.svg) we have\n![](images/90/90d5285154002b29f21dc2371e4d8cbe4680e683cc5cef46f0ca8677df1d2510.svg)\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n![](images/05/0531b1f1bc71a31d4cd672c8a78690516d9a68c81870bf1f0723d939e239f4d9.svg)\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ![](images/55/55883265eb6b2ec016ed0eb7afadd4e2d2d5183786e048f032d6d6e19f74a843.svg)\nfor all $n \\in \\mathbb{N}.$" :question/worked-solution "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove the following:\n\n![](images/83/833e64bb77220a52ec87ac4c333439ddf6097151aa992508070af2614a51ae36.svg)\n\nWe start by proving the **base case**, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side.\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n![](images/0f/0fd03d4f705ce8e2eb6969f528c847d274acd7ccaf816e2510f331ed8544314c.svg)\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the left-hand and right-hand sides independently and show they're equal.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some $n=k \\geq 1.$ To do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n).$\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n = \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}k \\geq 1},$ we have\n![](images/79/79ca61162feea82659e68725144768b96b947d21c7d0273f0435b07875b3b400.svg)\n\nThen, we move onto the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k) \\Rightarrow P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n![](images/11/11c05d918501f3e52784129b1125fc456d6f61b40f36124ca8666d206fee1c60.svg)\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this equation using the inductive hypothesis and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we're shown that $P(k) \\Rightarrow P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ![](images/df/dff83649ba6f41c29c1578c82159beff07d1355f4ad730dc4485e5af25de8a93.svg)\nfor all $n \\in \\mathbb{N}.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a47ce42d-7fbb-5f7c-999d-4c276632310b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083584 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84c497bf-fbac-5370-8739-261ba9c35136" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & k + 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080400} {:answer/id #