 We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}={{field-2}}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-3}}, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{{field-6}}={{field-7}}.\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore,\n${\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}{3}^{n} & \\frac{{3}^{n}-1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nfor all $n\\in N.$" :question/worked-solution "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}{3}^{n} & \\frac{{3}^{n}-1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the **base case**, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side.\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}{3}^{1} & \\frac{{3}^{1}-1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the left-hand and right-hand sides independently and show they're equal.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$ To do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n).$\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k}=\\begin{bmatrix}{3}^{k} & \\frac{{3}^{k}-1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nDue to the base case, we already know that the statement is true for some value of $k,$ namely $k=1,$ so this assumption is valid.\n\nThen, we move onto the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k+1}=\\begin{bmatrix}{3}^{k+1} & \\frac{{3}^{k+1}-1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this equation using the inductive hypothesis and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we're shown that $P(k)⇒P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore,\n${\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}{3}^{n} & \\frac{{3}^{n}-1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nfor all $n\\in N.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409786 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54ef93ae-39b9-5757-8300-eea0058de3a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5(k+1) & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409734} {:answer/id #uuid "77e5ea63-5fed-50b6-9dcb-f4c2f11bb088" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409738} {:answer/id #uuid "7c5fa8b6-63c2-53a6-8dea-a50e0f543c30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5\\,\\,k & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409741} {:answer/id #uuid "9f606721-6a03-5abe-94f2-d1b823a5ccbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ {0.5}^{k} & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409745}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5\\,\\,k & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409741} :answer-field/id #uuid "01904bbd-09cf-58ff-8b43-483564730398" :answer-field/key "fiel