trix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409754} :answer-field/id #uuid "6830d925-07b2-55b4-9da6-dfd7654706d5" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409778} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36ca2b13-d79d-550d-a843-cc4ace61f0aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5(0) & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409726} {:answer/id #uuid "6222caee-3214-57de-983a-a8b6c0cf57f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5+1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409736} {:answer/id #uuid "edd7ad0b-857d-5db9-a034-1689eba0aa7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5(1) & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409767} {:answer/id #uuid "f1f1786b-5978-5e78-bccd-ca48b2024403" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5(k) & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5(1) & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409767} :answer-field/id #uuid "69a630aa-9325-5b7b-8e3b-cad050cd5b86" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409779} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07cb646c-c85b-56c9-bdf0-ce1d53f9d356" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ {0.5}^{k+1} & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409717} {:answer/id #uuid "4832f9a3-5922-5385-934c-bd62674df66c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5k+1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409729} {:answer/id #uuid "4920eb09-52e0-52ad-b273-a72694e43af9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5k & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409730} {:answer/id #uuid "d6d09da4-0b4d-5956-9db5-99d9e639a583" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5(k+1) & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409761}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5(k+1) & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409761} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6289594-4e78-5c29-ae63-3c1f667cff4c" :answer-field/key "field-7" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409782}] :question/id #uuid "c2d53f1a-2ddc-5a46-80ab-ba5c178f639a" :question/math-academy-id "q-183767" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5n & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{for all }\\,n\\in N\n$$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}={{field-2}}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-3}}, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{{field-6}}={{field-7}}.\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore,\n${\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5n & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nfor all $n\\in N.$" :question/worked-solution "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5n & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the **base case**, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side.\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5(1) & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the left-hand and right-hand sides independently and show they're equal.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$ To do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n).$\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5\\,\\,k & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThen, we move onto the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k+1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5(k+1) & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this equation using the inductive hypothesis and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we're shown that $P(k)⇒P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore,\n${\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5n & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nfor all $n\\in N.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Proof Template"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083177 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082927 :question/id #uuid "d0ade273-b9ef-5c2d-bdcc-3f56f95e79ff" :question/math-academy-id "e-13238" :question/problem "Use mathematical induction to prove that\n![](images/07/07547d9d90fe3939d5ce9a528a2fd37a56980b566d17ee057436b6fcc4694fff.svg)\nfor all $n \\in \\mathbb{N}.$" :question/worked-solution "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1), P(2), P(3), \\ldots,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in\\mathbb N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in\\mathbb N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n![](images/d7/d7e3e5ed2dc60dd8a7a40711798ba589649a7a85cfb01630bdc0e63d779eee8e.svg)\n\nWe start by proving the base case, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that they're equal:\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ {4}^{1}-1 & {4}^{1}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\geq 1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\geq 1,$ we have\n![](images/fc/fc4407495fc6621f5e80daf8846c11206289d4be5fe4a8af9f67721291216a89.svg)\n\nWe must show that $P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the **inductive step**.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n![](images/7f/7fc2b8a2b2e0ec12f515afcb2d730adf2747ef3cea41f2c97d2d87df9e139118.svg)\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nWhen proving matrix identities using induction, the key to unlocking the proof is to factor out one instance of the matrix, as follows:\n\n![](images/09/099b3ed6a4c4dd958d1df9dbf507c775cf9a4a9f42ed661e5f8937f70cd46109.svg)\n\nLet's apply this technique in this case:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{k+1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{k}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{k}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ {4}^{k}-1 & {4}^{k}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ {4}^{k}-1+3\\cdot {4}^{k} & 4\\cdot {4}^{k}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4\\cdot {4}^{k}-1 & 4\\cdot {4}^{k}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ {4}^{k+1}-1 & {4}^{k+1}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ equals the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So let's write down our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction,\n![](images/f6/f67905088dd4d08876f93a46f2997497279c9eaadcdb4cc501ca932cb46265c2.svg)\nis true for all $n\\geq 1.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6befd731-de3f-545c-aca4-b51b67c68ace" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083585 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92096f41-55eb-5a4f-b044-1c188e9900c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080434} {:answer/id #uuid "c133b5d3-0fe9-5efb-a53c-b926350f5da4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\